>se

The Smokehouse
A squat hut of black timber, whose single room is a perpetual early evening.
Racks of curing meat hang in the smoke. A chopping block stands by the door with
a cleaver on it. The smoke finds your eyes in about a second and a half. A path
leads back northwest to the village green.

Dunna stands at the racks, watching the meat as though it might try something. 

>sharpen cleaver with stone
You work the cleaver along the stone with long, patient strokes until it sings.
Dunna comes over, tests the edge against a hair from her own arm, and is silent
for a respectful moment. "That'll do," she says. She whistles for her boy, who
runs off with a side of cured meat. "It's in your pit. Don't say I never gave
you anything."

[The score has just gone up by four points.]

>nw

The Village Green
