>cut cord with knife
You slide the knife under the cord and it parts with a sound like a shy cough.
The parcel can be opened now.

>open parcel
You open the brown parcel, revealing a handwritten note and a Guide Mk II.

[The score has just gone up by three points.]

>take guide
Taken.

>read note
In Ford's scrawl: "Arthur. Mind this for me. Don't switch it on, don't believe a
word it says and don't let it near anyone who wants to be helped. Will collect.
Soon-ish. F."

[The score has just gone up by two points.]

>switch on guide
The cube warms against your palm. A line of friendly text blooms on its face:
HELLO, ARTHUR. IT IS GOOD TO FINALLY BE SOMEWHERE WITH A VIEW. Beneath it, in
smaller type: YOU APPEAR TO BE HAPPY. THIS HAS BEEN NOTED.

>wait
Time passes.

A sound like tearing silk comes down out of the sky, and a sleek white shuttle
puts itself carefully down in the meadow. Presently two people come looking for
you: a woman, brisk and tired, and a girl who walks several paces behind with
her hands in her pockets as if she had been delivered against her will.

"Hello, Arthur," says the woman. You know her at once. Of all the people you did
not expect to see on Lamuella, she is the first. "This is Random. She's your
daughter."

She says it the way one reports a delay on a railway. The girl says nothing,
which she does with great force.

The cube, in your pocket, produces a tiny hum. A line of text writes itself on
its face where only you will see it: "A DAUGHTER. HOW NICE FOR YOU. THE GUIDE
HAS UPDATED YOUR ITINERARY."

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

>ask trillian about random
"Clever, furious and far too like both of us," says Trillian. "She was an
accident that took a great deal of paperwork."

[The score has just gone up by one point.]

>ask trillian about ford
Trillian's mouth tightens. "I've heard Ford's name near a very large amount of
trouble lately. Don't be any use to him."

[The score has just gone up by one point.]

>ask trillian about earth
"There is an Earth where I never left," says Trillian. "I look in on it
sometimes. I'm a good deal less happy there than you'd think."

[The score has just gone up by one point.]

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

Trillian consults something on her wrist that is not a watch. "I have to go,"
she says. "There is a story in another galaxy, and it won't wait. Look after
her. She is excellent at looking after herself, which is not the same thing."
She kisses her daughter on the top of the head, which Random permits as one
permits weather, and walks away up the meadow. A moment later the white shuttle
goes up like a breath being let out.

Random, who now has a father whom she has met for ten minutes, regards the hut
roof with extreme attention.

