>give sandwich to random
(first taking the sandwich)
Random inspects it with deep suspicion, bites, and holds very still. "Okay," she
says, with her mouth full, in a tone of grudging scientific honesty. "That's
actually good." It is the first thing you've given her that she has not
immediately turned into an insult.

[The score has just gone up by ten points.]

>wait
Time passes.

A rattling, wheezing noise announces that someone has landed a ship on the
meadow without much regard for its feelings. A little while later Ford Prefect
strides in, hair on end, towel over his shoulder.

"Arthur!" he says. "Good. You got it. Please tell me you didn't switch it on."
He glances at Random, then at you. "Ah. A great deal has happened since I last
checked. Sit down. Or don't."

>wait
Time passes.

>ask ford about guide
"It isn't finished," says Ford, "which is the only reason it's still dangerous.
The Mk II. It reads you while you read it. I mailed it to you because nobody in
the history of the Galaxy has ever looked for anything important in a sandwich
hut. No offence."

[The score has just gone up by two points.]

>ask ford about vogons
"They bought the Guide," says Ford. "Through a company with six names and one
address. I walked up to the address. Vogons, Arthur. Vogons in suits, which is
only Vogons with better excuses. They want Earth gone, all of it, in all its
versions, and this new edition is how they plan to be sure."

[The score has just gone up by one point.]

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

While you were listening to Ford, Random has done three things quietly: looked
at the ship on the meadow, worked out who it belongs to, and noticed that its
owner is distracted. By the time you notice, there is a noise from the meadow
like a large animal clearing its throat. Through the doorway you see a battered
ship wobble upward, pick up speed, and go.

Ford stares after it. "Of course she took my ship," he says. "Of course she
did." Then he is practical, which is the most alarming thing about him. "The
mail skiff. It's on the meadow with a pilot who is, as I understand it, hungry.
Make him something he wants and he'll fly us anywhere within reason. She'll head
for Earth, or what she thinks is Earth. We'll have to find out where that is."

"I COULD TELL YOU," writes the cube, helpfully, on its face. "BUT YOU DO NOT
SEEM TO WANT TO KNOW YET."

